1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,962
Range Requests Without Lying to Yourself.

2
00:00:02,962 --> 00:00:10,012
Quick, practical walkthrough for huge HTML on GitHub Pages or any CDN where compression is on by default.

3
00:00:10,012 --> 00:00:19,100
Hook: you `curl -r 0-4095 https://...` and the first 4 KB looks like gibberish.

4
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,687
We are going to show why and how to get trustworthy samples without guesswork.

5
00:00:23,687 --> 00:00:27,812
What Range really means: Range is bytes, not characters.

6
00:00:27,812 --> 00:00:32,187
With HTTP, Accept-Encoding influences which bytes you get.

7
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:37,412
Range is bytes; if the response is gzip, you're slicing compressed bytes.

8
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:40,850
That is the key idea to keep in your head as we sample.

9
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:46,387
The pitfall: when the server sends gzip, your range is taken over the compressed stream.

10
00:00:46,387 --> 00:00:54,900
The slice does not map to the top or bottom of the decoded HTML, so it looks corrupted or hides the version stamp you were hoping to spot.

11
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:58,225
Step 1: check headers before sampling.

12
00:00:58,225 --> 00:01:03,325
You want to see if the server supports ranges and whether it is compressing the response.

13
00:01:03,325 --> 00:01:04,037
Use:

14
00:01:04,037 --> 00:01:15,450
curl -sI https://user.github.io/site/ | egrep -i 'accept-ranges|content-encoding|content-length'

15
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:22,850
If you already see `Content-Encoding: gzip`, that tells you the bytes will be compressed unless you change your request.

16
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:27,287
Step 2: force identity so you are ranging the plain bytes.

17
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:30,150
Same probe, but with an explicit header:

18
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:41,800
curl -sI -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' https://user.github.io/site/ \

19
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:46,362
egrep -i 'accept-ranges|content-encoding|content-length'

20
00:01:46,362 --> 00:01:53,150
If the encoding disappears or shows identity, you know the server honored the request and you can range readable bytes.

21
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:57,362
Step 3: head sample once identity is confirmed.

22
00:01:57,362 --> 00:01:59,612
Fetch the first 4 KB:

23
00:01:59,612 --> 00:02:13,337
curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -r 0-4095 https://user.github.io/site/

24
00:02:13,337 --> 00:02:18,500
This should show a sane DOCTYPE, head tags, maybe a version string near the top.

25
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,462
Step 4: tail sample to see the end of the file:

26
00:02:22,462 --> 00:02:35,837
curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -r -4096 https://user.github.io/site/

27
00:02:35,837 --> 00:02:43,025
If you are sampling a huge HTML, this is where build IDs, closing tags, or integrity metadata usually live.

28
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:49,775
Optional verification: search both samples for version or build markers to detect split deployments quickly.

29
00:02:49,775 --> 00:03:04,762
curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -r 0-8191  https://... | rg -n 'version|build|id:'

30
00:03:04,762 --> 00:03:19,587
curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -r -8192 https://... | rg -n 'version|build|id:'

31
00:03:19,587 --> 00:03:25,662
If one side has a new build ID and the other does not, you are seeing mixed state, not necessarily a bad deploy.

32
00:03:25,662 --> 00:03:33,537
Safety and recap: head/tail samples are evidence of served bytes at that time, not proof of the whole document.

33
00:03:33,537 --> 00:03:36,100
Log the URL, time, and headers.

34
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:43,637
Range is bytes; if the response is gzip, you're slicing compressed bytes, so force identity before using byte ranges.

35
00:03:43,637 --> 00:03:47,062
That keeps your samples honest and stops the gibberish.

36
00:03:47,062 --> 00:03:51,837
Outro: link to repo and Video 1 for the broader mixed-state checklist.

